      "writtenOn": "2023-11-28T20:07:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Any guess how this (replacing memcpy with the smaller copying loop) is going to affect performance?\n\nSeparately: I know you called out the one obvious difference with referencing coords, but I do feel slightly queasy about that behavioral change -- this diverges from the GPU execution model now, with those lanes not being disabled. That could impact things like sampling (I guess those were always happening, though?). Is it really just explicit SkSL branches/loops that change behavior? I guess it means that branches based on sampled (eg image shader) results could behave differently?",
      "revId": "566dd5304c1d5eab9afa2d99002be6a828802b80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cd009b61_98f6240c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-28T20:32:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In the common case of 32-bit RGBA copying 4-byte pixels, I honestly expect it will be fine performance-wise. I have no real data (do we have a good way to bench this?). Memcpy with a small size that isn\u0027t known at compile time involves a bunch of branches; for huge copies memcpy can be clever, but for small copies without guaranteed alignment I don\u0027t think memcpy is going to do anything special. (I tried stepping through the memcpy version from PS1 and it didn\u0027t seem very efficient to me.) ARM\u0027s copy loop was 9 instructions long and Intel\u0027s was similar. On Intel (but not ARM), the compiler actually emitted an unrolled 8-element copy of the loop as well, which was only 6 instructions per byte/word; when this hits it\u0027ll be quite efficient.\n\nI am not sure how far this actually drifts from the GPU execution model, since we discard the out-of-bounds lanes in `restore_memory_contexts` making them essentially dead. Even in today\u0027s world, we could have code like:\n\n`if (coords.x \u003e\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 coords.x \u003c\u003d 10) color \u003d tex.eval(coords);`\n\nAnd this would still sample `tex` outside of the 0~10 coordinate range, wouldn\u0027t it? The value would be discarded afterwards, though, which is conceptually the same as what we are doing here.",
      "parentUuid": "0b6c1ac3_afc9f40b",
      "revId": "566dd5304c1d5eab9afa2d99002be6a828802b80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d08d6c98_ace2b33d",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-28T20:38:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was picturing something like: `if (child.eval(coords).a \u003e 0.5) { doSomethingExpensive(); }`, where you \"know\" that it\u0027s a rare case in reality, but the new edge handling means those lanes are still active, but sampling further off the edge of `child`. That\u0027s still just a performance issue, not a correctness one, though. (This is different from the GPU model, where those lanes will be masked off at the start, like we used to do in `init_lane_masks`). Should be fine, though - and simplifies the stage signature, which is a big plus.",
      "parentUuid": "cd009b61_98f6240c",
      "revId": "566dd5304c1d5eab9afa2d99002be6a828802b80",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
